<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Crisis</p><h1 class="enc-title">Agadir Crisis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1911">1911</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Second Moroccan Crisis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agadir">Agadir</a> Crisis, or Second Moroccan Crisis, began in July 1911 when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> sent the gunboat Panther to the Moroccan port of Agadir in protest against the French occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and in November 1911 Germany accepted a French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> in return for territory from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-equatorial-africa">French Equatorial Africa</a>, added to Kamerun as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neukamerun">Neukamerun</a>. The crisis strengthened the Anglo-French entente before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/agadir-crisis.jpg" alt="Agadir Crisis" width="320" height="195" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SMS_Panther.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>'s pre-eminence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> had been upheld by the Algeciras Conference of 1906, after the First Moroccan Crisis. On 9 February 1909 France and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> agreed that France would have exclusive political control while each upheld the other's economic interests. In 1911 a rebellion broke out against Sultan Abd al-Hafid. After forcing him to request help, the French prepared to send troops to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a>, on the pretext of protecting European lives and property, though the danger was remote, and dispatched a flying column at the end of April. The German move was aimed at testing the relationship between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and France and at winning compensation for accepting French control.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The French finance minister Joseph Caillaux told German diplomats in May that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> was prepared to make concessions elsewhere. On 1 July the gunboat SMS Panther arrived at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agadir">Agadir</a> under the pretext of protecting German trade, and the cruiser SMS Berlin followed days later. On 15 July Germany demanded, as compensation for a French protectorate, the whole French Congo from the Sangha River to the sea.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> at first thought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> largely responsible and urged it to avoid hasty measures. The prospect of a German naval port on the Atlantic changed this, and Foreign Secretary Sir Edward Grey told the German ambassador that Britain would respond forcefully; battleships were sent. Chancellor of the Exchequer David Lloyd George gave his Mansion House speech in July, bypassing the majority of the Cabinet, which favoured non-intervention. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> was also hit by financial turmoil: the stock market fell by 30 per cent in a day and the Reichsbank lost a fifth of its gold reserves in a month. Secret talks between Caillaux and the Germans produced the Franco-German Accord of 4 November 1911.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Under the accord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>'s position in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> in return for 275,000 square kilometres of the Middle Congo, which became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neukamerun">Neukamerun</a>, part of Kamerun. Germany ceded a small area to the French colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a>. Caillaux's negotiations were leaked and he fell from office on 21 January 1912. By the Treaty of Fes of 30 March 1912 France established a full <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> over Morocco, which lasted until 1956. Neukamerun returned to France in 1916.</p><p>The crisis strengthened the Entente between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, increased Anglo-German estrangement and led to a secret naval agreement between Britain and France. It also led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill">Winston Churchill</a> to conclude that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> must convert from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> to oil.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Agadir Crisis” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the <b>Agadir crisis</b>, France forced Sultan Abd al-Hafid of the Alaouite Sultanate to sign the Treaty of Fez in March 1912, establishing a protectorate.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-protectorate-in-morocco">French protectorate in Morocco</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Agadir crisis</b> of 1911 France ceded large territories, called Neukamerun, from French Equatorial Africa.</blockquote><a href="/state/kamerun">Kamerun</a></li></ul></section>
